shapeBodyT -- THE PER-SHAPE ROW ON THE KANT ROAD (SEQ 191 stroke 1, 2026-09-26).

WHAT IT REPLACES. The C++ emitter's rung targets (rung12, rung4, rung5, rung6, rung7, driven by
genScratch) pinned the C++ text genParse emitted for each term shape. This asks the same thing of
the generator that is alive: parser(), the kant road.

THE SHAPES, the Scaf family with LABELLED literals (a bare "x"- generates as GrouP()):
  literal      ShLit   kx-="x"                          (rung12's Scaf)
  reference    ShRef   ShA kb-="b"                      (rung4's ScafB)
  repetition   ShRep   ShA+ kc-="c"                     (rung5's ScafC)
  optional     ShOpt   ke-="e" ShA? kf-="f"             (rung6's ScafE)
               ShOptL  kf2-="f" kcm?-="," kg-="g"       (rung6's ScafF, optional literal)
  alternation  ShOUT   kl-="(" ShALT kr-=")"            (rung7's ScafALT inside ScafOUT)

TWO HALVES, AND THE SECOND IS NOT DECORATION. The generated BODY is pinned as text. But the body
does not carry the modifier: ShRep reads `ShA() && kc()` and ShOpt `ke() && ShA() && kf()`, the
same as a plain reference, because + and ? live on the term's own parse state and act at run
time. So a body pin alone cannot tell repetition or optional from reference. The DRIVES can:
each shape is told an accepting and a rejecting input on the OLD road, then again after parser().

THE READING, 2026-09-26: every body generates and compiles; every drive agrees across the roads
EXCEPT ONE, pinned by name --
  ShRep aaac   OLD matched 1 consumed 4   NEW matched 0 consumed 0
A generated body does not REPEAT a repeated reference. Candidate mechanism, NOT measured: the
body calls ShA() by name, which reaches the rule, while the + sits on ShRep's own term. Reported
as a finding, not fixed.
